# =============================================================================
# CiviCRM Standalone dev box  --  JPS / Cloud Scripting manifest
# =============================================================================
# The "install image". Install it via the Dashboard:
#   Dashboard > Import > Import manifest, then paste this URL:
#   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RaggedStaff/civi-dev-box/main/jps/civi-standalone.jps
#
# There is no `jps` CLI - JPS is the manifest format, not a command. For a
# scripted install see "Deploying" in the README.
#
# Target: app.jpe.infomaniak.com (Jelastic PaaS, public provider)
# Site:   https://dev.civi.sioldata.com
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ARCHITECTURE: WHY THIS USES CUSTOM IMAGES
#
# Both nodes are Docker images rather than the provider's certified stacks:
#
#   cp    - built from civicrm/civicrm-base:php8.5, in this repo's Dockerfile.
#           That base is Debian 12 / amd64, both of which are inside the
#           platform's supported base-OS allowlist, and it already provides
#           PHP 8.5.11, intl compiled in, DocumentRoot /var/www/html and
#           AllowOverride All.
#   sqldb - the official mariadb image, whose MARIADB_USER / MARIADB_PASSWORD /
#           MARIADB_DATABASE variables create the database and user on first
#           start. No bootstrap script is needed.
#
# An earlier version of this manifest used the certified PHP stack and a
# mariadb-dockerized node, and could not be installed. Six separate failures
# were all properties of those images rather than of anything in this repo:
#
#   1. intl shipped disabled. CiviCRM requires it, so preflight refused.
#   2. The provider's /etc/php.ini had a syntax error on line 688, and PHP
#      discards every directive after a parse error - silently, while `php -m`
#      still exits 0 and prints a normal module list.
#   3. `php --ini` printed its paths wrapped in double quotes, so a parser that
#      split on the colon captured '"/etc/php.ini"' and wrote to a file of
#      that name in the working directory.
#   4. The document root was /var/www/html, not the path the scripts assumed.
#   5. The Apache template shipped with HTTP Basic Auth on the document root.
#   6. The platform generates the database root password, and does not expose
#      it where a provisioning script can rely on it.
#
# A custom image removes that class of problem rather than working around it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PHP / CIVICRM VERSIONS
#   PHP 8.5.11 matches what dfc_civicrm permits (php_compatibility 8.1-8.5) and
#   what CiviCRM pairs with releases 6.16+ ("8.5 with 6.16+"). dfc_civicrm
#   permitted 8.5 only from 2026-10-08; before that 8.4 was acting as a ceiling
#   because the reference info.xml on docs.civicrm.org listed only 8.1-8.4.
#
#   The PHP version is baked into the image. The CIVICRM version is NOT - it is
#   fetched at install time, so one image serves several CiviCRM versions.
#
# WHAT THIS MANIFEST DOES *NOT* DO
#   It does not deploy the extension. The extension has no git remote and its
#   vendor/ + composer.lock are gitignored, so there is nothing to fetch here.
#   It is deployed afterwards as a built archive - see the README and
#   `make ext-install` / `make ext-verify`.
# =============================================================================
type: install
version: 1.0
name: CiviCRM Standalone Dev Box (dfc_civicrm)
description: |
  CiviCRM Standalone for testing the dfc_civicrm native extension.

  * PHP 8.5.11 (Apache + mod_php), intl compiled in
  * MariaDB 12.3, utf8mb4, CiviCRM's documented sql_mode
  * private/, public/ and ext/ on a persistent volume; survive redeploy
  * Demo data is a deployment-time choice

  Both nodes are custom Docker images; see the architecture note above for why.
homepage: https://dev.civi.sioldata.com
appVersion: 6.18.2
displayName: ${settings.envName}
skipNodeEmails: true

categories:
  - cms
  - php
  - crm

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment-time choices
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
settings:
  fields:
    - caption: Environment name
      type: string
      name: envName
      default: civi-dev
      hideLabel: false
      description: Name shown in the dashboard for this environment.

    - caption: Site URL
      type: string
      name: siteUrl
      default: "https://dev.civi.sioldata.com"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Baked into the install by `cv core:install --cms-base-url`. Must be the
        final stable URL - changing it later means re-installing.

    - caption: Application image
      type: string
      name: appImage
      default: "raggedstaff/civi-dev-box:8.5.11"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Built by this repo's Dockerfile. It MUST be pullable by the platform:
        push it to a registry first (`make image-push IMAGE=...`), or add a
        private registry in the dashboard under Custom templates.

        Its base is civicrm/civicrm-base:php8.5, which is Debian 12 / amd64 -
        both inside the platform's supported base-OS list.

    - caption: Database image
      type: string
      name: dbImage
      default: "mariadb:12.3"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        The official MariaDB image. Its base is Ubuntu 24.04, which is also
        inside the supported list. MARIADB_DATABASE / _USER / _PASSWORD below
        create the database and user on first start, so no bootstrap script is
        needed.

    - caption: MariaDB root password
      type: password
      name: dbRootPass
      default: civiRoot!2026
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Ours, not the platform's. Only used to administer the database by hand
        (`make db-root`). The application connects as the civiRM user below.

    - caption: Database password
      type: password
      name: dbPass
      default: civiDb!2026
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Supplied rather than generated so the database node and the PHP node
        use the same credentials without cross-node plumbing.

    - caption: Install CiviCRM demo (sample) data
      type: checkbox
      name: demoData
      default: false
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Off = empty site, installs in ~1 minute. On = realistic
        contacts/contributions to test against, much slower.

    - caption: CiviCRM version
      type: string
      name: civicrmVersion
      default: 6.18.2
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Downloaded at install time, so it is independent of the image tag.
        Verified to exist upstream.

    - caption: PHP cloudlets
      type: spinner
      name: cpCloudlets
      increment: 2
      default: 8
      min: 4
      description: CiviCRM is memory hungry. 8 recommended, 4 is the minimum.

    - caption: MariaDB cloudlets
      type: spinner
      name: dbCloudlets
      increment: 2
      default: 4
      min: 4

    - caption: Administrator email
      type: string
      name: adminEmail
      default: admin@sioldata.com
      hideLabel: false

    - caption: Administrator password
      type: password
      name: adminPass
      default: civiAdmin!2026
      hideLabel: false

    - caption: Persistent data directory
      type: string
      name: dataDir
      default: /var/lib/civicrm-data
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        private/, public/ and ext/ are symlinked here by 20-fetch-civicrm.sh,
        so uploads, civicrm.settings.php and installed extensions survive a
        container replacement.

    - caption: Extension key
      type: string
      name: extKey
      default: "dfc_civicrm"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Must equal the <key> in the extension's info.xml AND the directory name
        CiviCRM resolves to (<ext-dir>/<key>/<file>.php).

    - caption: Extension source (optional - usually leave blank)
      type: string
      name: extSource
      default: ""
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Only for a remote-pushed copy of the extension. dfc_civicrm has no git
        remote and its vendor/ is gitignored, so prefer the archive workflow:
        `make ext-install`. Blank = deploy the site only.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Topology
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
nodes:
  # Custom image, not the certified apache/php stack - see the architecture note.
  - image: ${settings.appImage}
    nodeGroup: cp
    displayName: CiviCRM
    cloudlets: ${settings.cpCloudlets}
    scalingMode: STATELESS
    diskLimit: 20
    isRedeploySupport: true
    isDeploySupport: false
    volumes:
      - ${settings.dataDir}
    links:
      - sqldb:DB
    env:
      CIVICRM_DATA_DIR: ${settings.dataDir}
      CIVICRM_VERSION: ${settings.civicrmVersion}
      CIVICRM_SITE_URL: ${settings.siteUrl}
      CIVICRM_DB_NAME: civicrm
      CIVICRM_DB_USER: civicrm
      CIVICRM_DB_PASS: ${settings.dbPass}
      CIVICRM_DB_PORT: "3306"
      CIVICRM_ADMIN_USER: admin
      CIVICRM_ADMIN_PASS: ${settings.adminPass}
      CIVICRM_ADMIN_EMAIL: ${settings.adminEmail}
      CIVICRM_DEMO_DATA: ${settings.demoData}
      CIVICRM_EXT_KEY: ${settings.extKey}
      CIVICRM_EXT_SOURCE: ${settings.extSource}

  # Official MariaDB image. MARIADB_DATABASE / _USER / _PASSWORD create the
  # database and user on first start, which is why there is no bootstrap script.
  - image: ${settings.dbImage}
    nodeGroup: sqldb
    displayName: MariaDB
    cloudlets: ${settings.dbCloudlets}
    diskLimit: 20
    scalingMode: STATEFUL
    isRedeploySupport: true
    isDeploySupport: false
    volumes:
      - /var/lib/mysql
    env:
      MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD: ${settings.dbRootPass}
      MARIADB_DATABASE: civicrm
      MARIADB_USER: civicrm
      MARIADB_PASSWORD: ${settings.dbPass}
      MARIADB_AUTO_UPGRADE: "1"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provisioning
#
# Scripts are fetched over HTTP from ${baseUrl}. Point baseUrl at a public raw
# URL for this repo, or override it as an extra setting on the API import call
# (see "Deploying" in the README).
#
# WITHOUT a reachable baseUrl the environment still creates; only the
# provisioning steps are skipped. You can then run the same scripts over SSH:
#   make provision
#
# Scripts MUST be run as files, not piped into `bash -s`. A script read from
# stdin has no BASH_SOURCE, so every script's `. lib.sh` line resolves to
# "/lib.sh" and the run dies with "BASH_SOURCE[0]: unbound variable".
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
baseUrl: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raggedstaff/civi-dev-box/main/

onInstall:

  # --- 0. Fetch the scripts ------------------------------------------------
  # curl || wget, and `|| exit 1`: a silent fetch failure leaves a zero-byte
  # script that fails later with a much more confusing message.
  - cmd [cp]: d="$HOME/.civi-scripts"; mkdir -p "$d"; for f in lib.sh 20-fetch-civicrm.sh 30-install-civicrm.sh 90-healthcheck.sh; do curl -fsS "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" -o "$d/$f" || wget -qO "$d/$f" "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" || exit 1; done

  # --- 1. Release code into the document root + bind volumes --------------
  # The image already provides intl, php.ini, the document root and the
  # private//core/ deny rules, so there is nothing to discover or repair here.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/20-fetch-civicrm.sh"

  # --- 2. Non-interactive install -----------------------------------------
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/30-install-civicrm.sh"

# The app tree is ephemeral, so re-materialise the release code on every restart.
# Data volumes are untouched, so this is cheap and idempotent.
onAfterRestartNode [cp]:
  - cmd [cp]: d="$HOME/.civi-scripts"; mkdir -p "$d"; for f in lib.sh 20-fetch-civicrm.sh; do curl -fsS "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" -o "$d/$f" || wget -qO "$d/$f" "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" || exit 1; done
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/20-fetch-civicrm.sh"

startPage: ${settings.siteUrl}/civicrm/login

success: |
  ### CiviCRM dev box is ready

  - **Site**: ${settings.siteUrl}/civicrm/login
  - **Admin**: `${settings.adminEmail}` / the admin password you chose
  - **CiviCRM**: ${settings.civicrmVersion} &nbsp;&nbsp; **Demo data**: ${settings.demoData}

  #### 1. Confirm private/ is not web-readable

      curl -sI ${settings.siteUrl}/private/civicrm.settings.php   # expect 403

  The image ships that deny rule, so it is already in place.

  #### 2. Deploy the extension (not done by this manifest)

  The extension is deployed as a built archive, because dfc_civicrm has no git
  remote and its `vendor/` is gitignored. From your workstation:

      make ext-build      # runs the extension's own tools/build-release.sh
      make ext-install    # uploads the archive and runs cv ext:enable
      make ext-verify     # runs the extension's own tools/verify-install.sh

  #### 3. Add cron (one-time, not scriptable from JPS)

  Dashboard > CUSTOMER > Cron Jobs, using `ops/crontab`.

  cron itself is installed and running in the image, so this is the only
  remaining step. Without it, scheduled mailings and the job queue are inert.